-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **CoordConv Misuse:** Using CoordConv layers in the discriminator for datasets with non-aligned objects (e.g., flowers, birds) degrades performance [S1].
-*   **Ray Density:** Processing time and memory increase with the number of rays. Using stratified sampling is more efficient than fixed rays; approximately 10 rays are needed for performance comparable to stratified sampling with fewer rays [S1].
-*   **Resolution Scaling:** For higher-resolution images, the number of points along the ray must be increased to maintain quality [S1].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **CoordConv Usage:** Using CoordConv layers in the discriminator for non-aligned objects (flowers, birds) degrades performance [S1].
+*   **Ray Density:** Processing time and memory increase with the number of rays. While 10 rays were found sufficient for comparable performance to stratified sampling with fewer rays, higher resolutions may require more points per ray [S1].
+*   **Unit Reduction:** Reducing synthesis network units from 256 to 128 is critical for allowing more points per ray, which improves performance [S1].
 
